{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1507223-what-is-the-problem-with-nephandi","id":1507223,"title":"What is the problem with Nephandi?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-04-02T14:53:25-07:00","id":1507223,"title":"What is the problem with Nephandi?","content":"I keep reading about how much toxic, horrible, divisive are Nephandi and how much players should <b>not</b> roleplay them.<br /> Ok, the problem with evil characters is an old one, but there seems to bee a bit... <i>too much</i> emphasis on them. We roleplay low humanity vampires, even Slashers , Baali, infernalists...<br /> <br /> So why the nephandi get the stick so hard?<br /> <br /> And, essentially, how is a nephandus different from an evil ordinary mage that uses magic without scruples for personal gain?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8595-conradine","name":"Conradine","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8595_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-02T15:18:17-07:00","id":1507227,"title":"","content":"The closest thing to a Nephandus I've ever seen is a Scelesti (Mage: the Awakening) but my guess is that it's because they are striving to literally unmake Creation.<br /> <br /> As in the Universe, <i>all</i> of it,not just the World of Darkness.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3201-father-enoch","name":"Father Enoch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3201_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"285"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-02T16:21:59-07:00","id":1507235,"title":"","content":"I think there's a bit of conflation of different Internet debates going on here.<br /> <br /> A lot of the division around the Nephandi has nothing to do with how playable they are, but how they got using in the Rev. and M20 metaplot. <br /> <br /> The &quot;you should never ever play these&quot; is also something that largely comes from specific Mage developers that aren't particularly big fans of playing Sabbat, Black Spirals, or other overtly evil characters types that were created solely to be the dark black to the PCs shades of grey. Which isn't nearly as common of a sentiment within the larger Mage community.<br /> <br /> The question of how is a Nepandus different from just an evil mage? The Nephandus is definitionally worse. In the race to the bottom where Nephandi have to be worse than just being evil, the interesting parts of playing an evil character gets lost in the transgressive high of being ever more atrocious; something that inevitably leads to someone's lines getting crossed and the game getting unfun.<br /> <br /> I would say the books have a similar stance on the Baali as not really being good player characters too for that matter.<br /> <br /> A lot of the evil-but-playable character types have some interesting themes and things to explore because they generally have a point, but their methods are both an internal and external source of diminishing what's right about their positions.<br /> <br /> The Sabbat, for example, aren't interesting because of the blood-rave-orgies. They're interesting because they accept the World of Darkness for what it really is far more than the other major vampire factions. They're interesting because of the contradictions and paradoxes that stem from how their founding ideals clash with the realities of being a group fighting a war over generations. They're interesting because in the end they really don't have any way to prove that just because they know that &quot;truth&quot; more than the others, that they've actually come up with a good solution to things; there is a pretty solid case that their methods are actually making the things they hate about the world worse, while the Camarilla's comforting lies might actually be better from a utilitarian standpoint.<br /> <br /> The Nephandi as PCs invariably kill that sort of nuance. The closest you get is different groups of them squabbling over the best way to destroy the world. There's no Code of Milan that creates philosophic struggles with real stakes within Nephandi groups. There's no unifying reason for Nephandi of differing persuasions to actually work together either.<br /> <br /> This leaves them as only really interesting to use as NPCs because as NPCs they serve to add drama and conflict to the story of the PCs. They don't have enough of their own to really last as PCs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":10}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-02T17:41:42-07:00","id":1507245,"title":"","content":"In addition to <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> ' excellent treatment, it's also a matter of scale. Nephandi are not evil in the &quot;<i>I'm a selfish amoral bastard that does whatever he pleases and doesn't believe in scruples</i>&quot; kind of way. There are many characters belonging to all the factions of the different critters in the WoD that fall under this category. They're normal for their context even among the traditionally &quot;positive&quot; factions of the WoD like the Traditions and the Garou Nation.<br /> <br /> The trick with Nephandi is that they are not people with a lack of morals. They are a group of utterly insane deranged monstrosities that see horrifying deeds, corruption and debasement of people around them as light entertainment and setting the mood for a world either ending or being handed over to things from beyond. Worse yet, they chose this of their own free will and corrupted a part of their soul to go on and make everyone who comes after them take up their crusade against the state of Being.<br /> <br /> There is little to make these characters valid in play beyond the excesses the players can imagine them performing on others, because at the end of the day they will never evolve or care about anything other than suffering and the ultimate corruption of the world.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-02T20:05:01-07:00","id":1507254,"title":"","content":"Something my wife thought of as a pop-culture example here:<br /> <br /> The Nephandi are a lot like the Cenobite from the Hellraiser franchise. There isn't really anything for them to &quot;do&quot; besides purge themselves of any lingering humanity, and then engage in ever gorier &quot;experiences&quot; with their victims. They lack real agency as well, as they've essentially subjucated themselves to the Leviathan and the rules that decide when they can access Earth and who they can take to their realm(s). Playing Pinhead would be boring. Not because he's a bad character as an antagonist, but as a protagonist what do you do? Wait around for someone to unseal the configuration, then torment then until you get bored. Then, maybe, someone actually does something a bit novel and you get to kill a lot of people as your one diversion?<br /> <br /> Would you actually want to play that?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-02T21:21:37-07:00","id":1507261,"title":"","content":"Partly the Nephandi are considered off-limits by game designers who don't want to make &quot;Nephandi: The Atrocity&quot; a base playstyle. The Nephandi aren't &quot;fun&quot; evil - the kind that cackles &quot;Mwuh Ha Ha!&quot; and wears all black. Rather, they're the serious, &quot;no one would have fun at a table where you do the kind of things Nephandi do recreationally&quot; evil. (Unless the players were deeply, profoundly disturbed). The kind of shit that comes with a Content Warning is standard practice for them. Done for no reason but because they want to, or to please some entity from beyond the world.<br /> <br /> It's not that you can't - no one can really stop you - but rather you <i>shouldn't</i>.<br /> <br /> Nor is there any point or rationale for it, when it comes to Nephandi. It's my headcanon that Nephandi don't serve extra-planar beings as a goal unto itself. Rather, Demons or the Wyrm or the Great Ones are a means to an end. Not even necessarily the world's end. They're just shackled, emotionally, to Descent. They do it because, with their broken and inverted souls, breaking and killing and crippling and soiling and corrupting and making people suffer IS the point. They just like it. Anything else is an after-the-fact rationalization of a deeply irrational antipathy to Reality. If they can destroy or ruin it in a final way, they would. But they want, first and foremost, to spoil it all for everyone else.<br /> <br /> That sort of brings it around to a very good reason Nephandi shouldn't be a player option: it's a deeply anti-social, unmutual path. Not good for a group play experience. It is the worst stereotype of Chaotic Evil play in D&amp;D. It's <i>disruptive</i>. By definition, it promotes negative play experiences as the one and only playstyle.<br /> <br /> At least with the Sabbat, there's in-built mechanisms within the narrative for the Pack staying together and collaborating. Sabbat players can have fun being monsters, without stepping on each others' toes. And at least the Sabbat's evil isn't JUST in service to their gratification. The sect has goals and fascinating philosophies about what it means to be a vampire. At least the Baali, while frequently working with or for Demons (or Sleepers), have a certain level of moral agency.<br /> <br /> If you could sign up to play a Nephandus, you're playing the worst kind of evil, who has no choice in the matter.<br /> <br /> <br /> And what are you getting out of a Nephandi PC, that can't arise from a PC that is &quot;merely&quot; a bastard? Nothing. You can just play a bad person, with selfish motives and delusions of grandeur. You can just play a &quot;Dark Mage&quot;. You'll likely have way more fun, since you can define what being the antihero or villain protagonist means to you. As many D&amp;D parties have shown, you CAN have a token Evil teammate, and still function as a play group.<br /> <br /> It's when you use &quot;Alignment: Evil&quot; as a blank check to disrupt play that's the actual problem. By definition, Nephandi are disruptive. They HAVE to be, to be Nephandi.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-03T16:03:10-07:00","id":1507322,"title":"","content":"The biggest reason I can see against playing them is they're not very interesting as pcs despite being excellent antagonists.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 04-04-2023, 03:41 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-03T16:06:55-07:00","id":1507323,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ragged Robin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1507223-what-is-the-problem-with-nephandi?p=1507322#post1507322\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The biggest reason I can see against playing them is they're not very intestine as pcs despite being excellent antagonists.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed. Beings lacking full free will are normally dull to roleplay.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-09T03:14:50-07:00","id":1507849,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1507223-what-is-the-problem-with-nephandi?p=1507245#post1507245\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In addition to <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> ' excellent treatment, it's also a matter of scale. Nephandi are not evil in the &quot;<i>I'm a selfish amoral bastard that does whatever he pleases and doesn't believe in scruples</i>&quot; kind of way. There are many characters belonging to all the factions of the different critters in the WoD that fall under this category. They're normal for their context even among the traditionally &quot;positive&quot; factions of the WoD like the Traditions and the Garou Nation.<br /> <br /> The trick with Nephandi is that they are not people with a lack of morals. They are a group of utterly insane deranged monstrosities that see horrifying deeds, corruption and debasement of people around them as light entertainment and setting the mood for a world either ending or being handed over to things from beyond. Worse yet, they chose this of their own free will and corrupted a part of their soul to go on and make everyone who comes after them take up their crusade against the state of Being.<br /> <br /> There is little to make these characters valid in play beyond the excesses the players can imagine them performing on others, because at the end of the day they will never evolve or care about anything other than suffering and the ultimate corruption of the world.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem, in my opinion, is that Mage does not offer this &quot;path&quot;.<br /> I can play a sabbat in VTM. I can portray a very jaded elder of the Camarilla. Or for that matter, a neonate/ancilia.<br /> You can not really do this in Ascension, however. In essence, Character sheets that would have been A-OK in vtm, Koe, Wto, CtD and so on would have resulted in your mage being, essentially, an outlaw.<br /> The law of the traditions makes attrocities towards muggles illegal, and not just out of a sense of pragmatism. We can debate the extend to which this law is followed, but unlike other splats, i get the impression that much of the moralizing of the traditions is not hypocricy, but the actual truth of how they operate. In fact, their Tradition books make it pretty clear.<br /> <br /> For a myriad of reason that would take us a year's worth of debates, you are really expected to play a hero of varying kinds. The books make it clear. So forget about, evil, you are not normally<br /> expected to even play an amoral politico.<br /> <br /> Honestly, my impression is that it was OOC considerations, that created the IC Nephandi rather than the opoosite. The caul, while philosophically interesting, all but screams &quot;You should not play them, did i make it clear enough??&gt;?&quot;. I could go on, but i think i've made my point clear. Hell, even the Baali are not all portrayed as seeking their descension. Moloch, for instance, was deeply amoral and even immoral, but pretty much considered the world ending &quot;a deal breaker&quot;.<br /> <br /> <br /> In Mage the Awakening, you have a choice. You can play a seer of the throne. (And no, the technocracy is NOT the seers, except maybe 1st edition technocracy).<br /> This does not exist in Ascension. Period. Its like there is a big gap somewhere. I think the closest equivalent are the way many Nephandi are portrayed in M20 and sorcerer's crusade, i.e not really interested in Descension, but merely &quot;bad people&quot; who would skin a child for some extra quintessence. (i.e a juice bag as the sabbat would say). For the record, i have not yet finished reading all of book of the fallen, but this seems to be their portrayal in &quot;Path of Screams&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-09T04:31:27-07:00","id":1507856,"title":"","content":"That's quite a myopic view <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1492_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1492\" data-vbnamecard=\"1492\">mark</a> - It absolutley does support things like the sabbat or the dharmas or even the unseelie. Mage allows you to validate and create all kinds of philosophical frameworks for your characters. It doesn't limit you on ethics in any way.<br /> <br /> It's incredibly weird that you can even consider the Sabbat in any way equivalent to the nephandi.<br /> <br /> What it doesn't approve is being an asshole who doesn't do anything but destroy humans and/or reality by debasing them just because.<br /> <br /> Mage tells you what it is, why you shouldn't do it and leaves you with the tools to do it, if you really want to.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-09T05:23:10-07:00","id":1507860,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1507223-what-is-the-problem-with-nephandi?p=1507856#post1507856\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's quite a myopic view <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1492_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1492\" data-vbnamecard=\"1492\">mark</a> - It absolutley does support things like the sabbat or the dharmas or even the unseelie. Mage allows you to validate and create all kinds of philosophical frameworks for your characters. It doesn't limit you on ethics in any way.<br /> <br /> It's incredibly weird that you can even consider the Sabbat in any way equivalent to the nephandi.<br /> <br /> What it doesn't approve is being an asshole who doesn't do anything but destroy humans and/or reality by debasing them just because.<br /> <br /> Mage tells you what it is, why you shouldn't do it and leaves you with the tools to do it, if you really want to.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, i know the philosophical differences between,say, the Nephandi and the Sabbat mind you. I am simply referring to &quot;evil&quot; from a practical perspective. The average tzimisce metamorphosist will probably hurt more people than certain Nephandi archetypes. <br /> <br /> Yes, it allows you to create &quot;philosophical frameworks&quot; in a mechanic sense, but i would argue that its style, its tone, its vibe is a lot more moralistic than that of most other gamelines . <br /> <br /> At any rate, my basic response to the OP is that the writers do not, for all number of reasons, want you to be playing a &quot;bad&quot; person.<br /> In all honestly, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8595-conradine\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar8595_3.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"8595\" data-vbnamecard=\"8595\">Conradine</a> there is no reason you should not be playing them. The wod was made back in the 90s shortly after the satanic moral panic of the 80s. This is the reason you can make a Tzimisce voivode that creates a Vozhd but you can not play a Baali. Its because it evokes images of demon worship, even though, the Baali do not necessarily do this. On the other side<br /> said Voivode does not do so, even if his motives are just as petty as they can get.<br /> Like, seriously, in real life i would like to avoid both people just as much. Even on a philosophical level, they are not necessarily better than each other if you ask me.<br /> <br /> Like i said, i have not finished all Book of the fallen, but its my impression that only some Nephandi are presented as Nihilists in the &quot;I seriously want to unmake the word&quot; sense. The other half seems to me like extreme social darwinists. <br /> <br /> That is, the same factions that seem to exist in clanbook Baali seem to sort of, implicitly be there in the Nephandi as well. Mind you, i am mostly basing this on Infernalism the path of screams so i don't know if something changed??<br /> <br /> I imagine that as the Traditions became more moral over the centuries, certain people who would have fit right in during the 1200s now go to the Nephandi.<br /> <br /> So, i prefer to portray the Nephandi as a somewhat uneasy Alliance between A) the actual Nihilistis, B) people like the circle of red, C) a fair number of the people presented in,say, &quot;House of Tremere&quot;.<br /> Only the first are, in my portrayal interested in this inversion thing.<br /> <br /> Mind you, this is an explicitly NON-canon view, but i think it better represents the spectrum of &quot;evil&quot;.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-09T09:44:18-07:00","id":1507873,"title":"","content":"Bear in mind that the barrier to playing a Nephandus is <i>entirely</i> philosophical. There are no game system barriers to play.<br /> \u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-09T09:53:13-07:00","id":1507874,"title":"","content":"Also: the Nephandi in <b>Book of the Fallen</b> aren't <i>really</i> about nihilism; they're about abuse. A common rationale for their abuse is that the world is inherently sick and needs a mercy killing (the Nephandic version of nihilism); but ultimately, the focus is on how the Nephandi like to tear people down \u2014 either directly through torture and the like, or indirectly by getting <i>others</i> to commit atrocities. \u201cSome people just want to watch the world burn\u201d is a better summary of them as most recently presented. <b>\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b</b>\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-09T11:34:42-07:00","id":1507879,"title":"","content":"It's to discourage chaotic stupid. <br /> <br /> As someone who's relatively unfazed by people narrating the grotesque, there is some interest in playing a nephandi: Not getting caught; deceiving other characters, not to mention players, is fun, and it can be fun for other players if you do it right. That said, it's really really difficult to play that kind of evil without making the game worse for others, and furthermore you could play a regular monster without needing to be a nephandi.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-09T11:38:18-07:00","id":1507880,"title":"","content":"I found a mention about some Nephandi searching some kind of redemption. Beside the question &quot;is redemption possible or not&quot;, since the Caul literally inverts and break the soul, is redemption something that a Nephandi can even conceptualize?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8595-conradine","name":"Conradine","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8595_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-09T11:40:25-07:00","id":1507881,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1507223-what-is-the-problem-with-nephandi?p=1507879#post1507879\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's to discourage chaotic stupid.<br /> <br /> As someone who's relatively unfazed by people narrating the grotesque, there is some interest in playing a nephandi: Not getting caught; deceiving other characters, not to mention players, is fun, and it can be fun for other players if you do it right. That said, it's really really difficult to play that kind of evil without making the game worse for others, and furthermore you could play a regular monster without needing to be a nephandi.</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly, remember that being a regular monster is illegal within the traditions, that is part of the problem.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-09T11:41:02-07:00","id":1507882,"title":"","content":"You are not your Avatar; so yes. Even if you're Avatar has been inverted, you can still struggle against its impulses. The key feature of Mage is that they have agency.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-09T12:42:31-07:00","id":1507890,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1507223-what-is-the-problem-with-nephandi?p=1507881#post1507881\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly, remember that being a regular monster is illegal within the traditions, that is part of the problem.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's what the law says, but there's limited actual oversight. I mean, the vast majority of potential victims (sleepers) aren't in a position to report abuse to a tribunal.<br /> <br /> <i>Book of Secrets</i> actually notes that the Tradition's justice system has repeatedly failed. Like, a large thread of Ascension's metaplot is that no one notices what Voormas is up to until it's almost too late.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-09T13:13:50-07:00","id":1507893,"title":"","content":"Focusing just on the Traditions is also forgetting that there are other playable factions. The Technocracy doesn't really like &quot;evil&quot; individuals, but is institutionally horrific on a scale few other things can match; even if you ignore all the NIC metaplot stuff. Playing a Technocrat isn't as fun in a transgressive sort of way as many of the other &quot;evil but playable&quot; PC types, but if we're using D&amp;D alignments broadly, it's a Lawful Evil organization, and PC Technocrats are going to end up having to come to term with that in some fashion.<br /> <br /> The Disparate Alliance, unlike the Traditions, doesn't have any centralized code of conduct. Each Craft within it is responsible for policing its own. Many of the core groups are more or else of a similar mind as the Traditions in regards to how to interact with human society, but the tentative and potential members have a large number of groups that are by no means &quot;nice.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-09T13:30:50-07:00","id":1507895,"title":"","content":"Plus, there are the claims that the Technocracy and/or Traditions leadership have been corrupted by the Nephandi.<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-09T20:26:06-07:00","id":1507916,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Conradine</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1507223-what-is-the-problem-with-nephandi?p=1507880#post1507880\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I found a mention about some Nephandi searching some kind of redemption. Beside the question &quot;is redemption possible or not&quot;, since the Caul literally inverts and break the soul, is redemption something that a Nephandi can even conceptualize?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think so, intellectually. A Nephandus is still a creature capable of reason.<br /> <br /> The bigger question is what would motivate a Nephandus to seek redemption? What would make them go against the current of their soul that seeks, at all times, to sink to the lowest depths possible?<br /> <br /> A while ago, I actually thought of one: <i>Spite</i>.<br /> <br /> Imagine you're a Widderslainte. You were born bad. Heir to a legacy of despair and malice. Enemy to all creation, who can never know true peace or contentment. While normal Mages have an apotheosis to seek, Nephandi like you have no such end state to achieve. Just more ruination, in an endless spiral downward. Always descending, never hitting the bottom. Ascension has been forever denied to you. All because a previous incarnation made a choice to forsake it.<br /> <br /> Would you not, then, feel bitter about that? Bitter against them, your past self, for stealing your godhood from you?<br /> <br /> Well <i>fuck that guy</i>. The greatest act of self-determination a Nephandus could make in this world or the next is to spoil that choice. As a Widderslainte, you're all too familiar with how easy it is to break things. The ultimate act of both worthiness and antipathy is to build something that makes that bastard irrelevant. To prove they were weak, and you are strong. Strong enough to break rules seemingly as old as time itself.<br /> <br /> Such a Nephandus would seek redemption - to repair their broken soul - out of SPITE, mother-fucker. THAT is extremely in keeping with their mentality.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":6156,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","user":"Bluecho","date":". 04-09-2023, 11:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-09T21:26:05-07:00","id":1507921,"title":"","content":"There's also a lot of &quot;Nephandi&quot; that are really just pawns of the more powerful Nephandi that they inducted just to toss to the wolves when the PC factions need to think they've caught and gotten rid of the Nephandus causing all their problems.<br /> <br /> The story of Mercedes/Amanda (the corrupt student of Senex who he tried to cure of being a Widderslainte between incarnations to possible success) basically follows along this: Mercedes was seduced by a Nephandus skilled enough to be operating under Senex's nose, and when Senex finally confronted her with everything she'd done thinking it was out of love for her companion, she balked and let Senex kill her; giving Senex the chance to try to save her Avatar. Amanda, while not out of spite, is trying to both live as a proper Euthanatos assassin, while also redeeming herself at least by her Tradition's standards in case Senex's skills only delayed her fall to her twisted Avatar instead of actually curing her.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-10T03:15:26-07:00","id":1507927,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1507223-what-is-the-problem-with-nephandi?p=1507890#post1507890\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's what the law says, but there's limited actual oversight. I mean, the vast majority of potential victims (sleepers) aren't in a position to report abuse to a tribunal.<br /> <br /> <i>Book of Secrets</i> actually notes that the Tradition's justice system has repeatedly failed. Like, a large thread of Ascension's metaplot is that no one notices what Voormas is up to until it's almost too late.</div> </div> </div> </div>Right, but what happens about the people who are bad enough that they do not agree with said laws?? That is, the people who did not vote for them for a lack of better word.<br /> The problem with mage is that it basically implies that there are literally more complete nihilists than there are people who are just plain bad. (i.e the aforementioned house of Tremere)<br /> <br /> That is, if i am wrong, then it begs the question of how the current law passed to begin with. <br /> <br /> Some posters seem to be implying that the answer is &quot;oh the law is just a hypocritical compromise&quot;* but i don't really see it. Remember that Voormas is an Archmage, so yeah, he is going to get away with a lot of stuff. Even he, however was basically sentenced in absentia once his crimes against sleepers became apparent (ok he also committed crimes against mages, but if i am supposed to conclude that the former would not have gotten him indicted, it's just not there).<br /> <br /> Likewise, the argument about &quot;it's very hard for sleepers to report evil mages or for them to get caught by others&quot; misses the point. Yes, it is very difficult in real life to &quot;catch&quot; rapists in the act, but most of us do genuinely care about it. That is, if we knew for a fact that they were guilty, we would indeed want them imprisoned.<br /> <br /> But an evil mage would not necessarily want you to get imprisoned for,say, abusing a sleeper. <br /> <br /> *Normally, i could get behind the idea that the law is hypocritical. A compromise of sorts. Some mages genuinely caring about sleepers, others don't but most of them not wanting a civil war about it. So you create a legal system that, on purpose, does not really work in this regard. It kinda makes sense in fact. Mages are always judged only by their own Tradition, unless other mages got hurt.<br /> There are very few mages, so you are most likely judged by people who kinda know you, or know someone who know you. People who think that it would be a damn shame if you got beheaded and you were no longer there to heal them from a life-threatening injury, like you did last Tuesday. After all, Awakened are a rare resource.<br /> The occasional asshole would be punished/executed Especially, if it just so happened that nobody liked him for political reasons. Or if he got too &quot;excited&quot; about gaming the system, and so on.<br /> This would result in a legal system that,say, reduces the amount of muggle victims, compared to,say, the 1200s, but not anywhere near the extend that it could have. (again, even on top of the practical difficulties of finding perpetrators and victims)<br /> <br /> I can get behind all that, but none of it is there. Or if it is, i'd like to see it.<br /> <br /> Thus, the only logical assumption, from a,say, psychological perspective, is that many of these people would join the Nephandi, that is, the Nephandi as they would be rather than as written.<br /> <br /> So, my answer to the OP is that its easier to play a Nephandus if <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8595-conradine\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar8595_3.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"8595\" data-vbnamecard=\"8595\">Conradine</a> you accept the above premise.<br /> Or like i said, a philosophical Baali/Nephandus is more interesting than half the Sabbat out there. It is just 80s leftovers that made it seem otherwise in the 90s, and,uhm, similar concerns that make it seem so today.<br /> Remember that a few of the writers are true believers,in a sense. They genuinely do think that infernal powers are real, at least to a certain extend. I am not writing this to mock them btw. It is just that this is the reason they tend to be more OK with Sabbat. Because the Sabbat do not really correspond to any real life infernal belief system(s) or anything like that.<br /> On the other hand, it is theoretically possible to find some Nephandi-like in the real world","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-10T15:06:21-07:00","id":1507962,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1507223-what-is-the-problem-with-nephandi?p=1507927#post1507927\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Right, but what happens about the people who are bad enough that they do not agree with said laws??</div> </div> </div> </div>Most of them, if they can't stay hidden or don't want to, become independent mages (this has even happened to &quot;good&quot; mages that got caught up on the wrong side of &quot;spirit of the law vs. letter of the law&quot; responses to their actions), or are members of Crafts/Disparates with laws more aligned with their moral beliefs.<br /> <br /> Your argument is forgetting that the Traditions do not have a 100% lock on all the mystic mages in the world. As a common example, when the Council of Nine Mystic Traditions was first created, <b>half</b> of the mages that would have been slotted into the Dreamspeakers just up and left without signing on. They saw a bunch of rules that served the more hegemonic minded Order of Hermes and Celestial Chorus (and to an extent the not much better Batini, Euthanoi, and Akashics), and wanted no part in the rules instead of bowing to the pressure to reform the rules over time from the inside. That's part of the reasons the Crafts are so heavily dominated by local indigenous culture focused mages. They decided not to vote for the rules by not joining the organization at all, so they could follow their own rules their own way in their own little corner of the world. <br /> <br /> Even the non-indigenous focused Crafts are generally groups that chaffed from the restrictions of the Tradition's organization-wide rules. The Hollowers really struggled with it, and as of M20's response to the Revised metaplot, have fully given up on the Tradition. Then you have radicals on the other side of things like the Templars that can't stand the Traditions forcing them to respect the validity and equality of non-Christian mages and refused to go along with the Chorus' shift towards a Unitarian mindset so... they didn't join the Traditions to stay their own thing.<br /> <br /> The setting has always had space in it for mages that don't feel like following the rules of the Traditions. Plenty of Crafts support things that the Traditions would consider violations of their rules. The Crafts are not a &quot;bad guy&quot; faction, but their philosophies are just as easily opposed to the Tradition's laws as not. A few of them are pretty bad though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-11T02:21:41-07:00","id":1508009,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1507223-what-is-the-problem-with-nephandi?p=1507962#post1507962\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Most of them, if they can't stay hidden or don't want to, become independent mages (this has even happened to &quot;good&quot; mages that got caught up on the wrong side of &quot;spirit of the law vs. letter of the law&quot; responses to their actions), or are members of Crafts/Disparates with laws more aligned with their moral beliefs.<br /> <br /> Your argument is forgetting that the Traditions do not have a 100% lock on all the mystic mages in the world. As a common example, when the Council of Nine Mystic Traditions was first created, <b>half</b> of the mages that would have been slotted into the Dreamspeakers just up and left without signing on. They saw a bunch of rules that served the more hegemonic minded Order of Hermes and Celestial Chorus (and to an extent the not much better Batini, Euthanoi, and Akashics), and wanted no part in the rules instead of bowing to the pressure to reform the rules over time from the inside. That's part of the reasons the Crafts are so heavily dominated by local indigenous culture focused mages. They decided not to vote for the rules by not joining the organization at all, so they could follow their own rules their own way in their own little corner of the world.<br /> <br /> Even the non-indigenous focused Crafts are generally groups that chaffed from the restrictions of the Tradition's organization-wide rules. The Hollowers really struggled with it, and as of M20's response to the Revised metaplot, have fully given up on the Tradition. Then you have radicals on the other side of things like the Templars that can't stand the Traditions forcing them to respect the validity and equality of non-Christian mages and refused to go along with the Chorus' shift towards a Unitarian mindset so... they didn't join the Traditions to stay their own thing.<br /> <br /> The setting has always had space in it for mages that don't feel like following the rules of the Traditions. Plenty of Crafts support things that the Traditions would consider violations of their rules. The Crafts are not a &quot;bad guy&quot; faction, but their philosophies are just as easily opposed to the Tradition's laws as not. A few of them are pretty bad though.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, you can become an independent mage. That is definitely an option. The crafts are not. I am, say, a Hermetic/Etherite so joining the wu lung is simply not a viable thing for me.<br /> Also, remember that i am not talking about &quot;well intentioned extremist&quot; kind of evil, or even hypocritical evil. <br /> It is also not an option to go back to the year 1200, or to wait till i am so powerful that the rules do not really apply<br /> <br /> I assume that mages who are just immoral/amoral (but not unstable) are more, in terms of quantity, than those whose dream is to undo the freaking universe.<br /> This is what i mean when i say that there is a &quot;missing&quot; faction. Sort of making up one, i see only 2 viable options<br /> A)Many of the traditions laws would be a lot more hypocritical than they are actually presented. That is, modern &quot;House of Tremere&quot; mages would be a bit more discreet, a bit more restrained, but still there.&quot;<br /> B)This compromise would be refused and a lot of these people would instead be pushed into an alliance with the Nephandi. <br /> In other words, exactly the factions presented in, say, clanbook baali, save that there would be one more called &quot;Casual assholes&quot;, also known as &quot;Please buy &quot;House of Tremere&quot;&quot;<br /> <br /> Example<br /> * Bob is a mage. Bob kinda likes his friends. He probably has rivalries with some of his fellow magi, but on the whole he has at least some loyalty to his group, to a lesser or greater extend. Bob is working on a spell, based on some fleshly thing (vozhd) that he saw some vampires doing. Of course, this is paradoxical as hell, but he is mostly interested in it from a research perspective. Now, Bob is using a few serial killers and rapists that he &quot;borrowed&quot; from the local police.<br /> <br /> Or, for a twist, Bob now starts using people who &quot;are the scum of society&quot; They will not be missed. He is probably telling himself that he is doing society a favor by giving them &quot;purpose&quot;<br /> <br /> Evil as hell, right? That is, i really don't want to get close to this guy. I assume that even the first option (with the rapists/serial killers) would be some level of illegal in most Traditions.<br /> <br /> But the point is that Bob exists. He is not really a metaphysical nihilist. Presumably, he is less rare than actual omnicidal maniacs. <br /> So, where is Bob? In the books he is practically non-existent, and this is why i think it makes sense that he would go to the Nephandi.* I mean, either that or he will be doubly eager to prove his loyalty to the Traditions that he might use his inventions to hunt Nephandi. If i am a Nephandus, option number one is the best for me.<br /> I just don't see a huge crown of Nephandi, trying to run him out due to his lacking &quot;purity&quot;<br /> <br /> How on earth will his every day actions be different compared to a Nephandus trying to achieve (personal) Descension? <br /> Of course, Bob is not interested in Ascension either. He simply wants power for power's own sake, he simply has extremely few morals, especially in regards, to, say, sleepers. To be honest, i think the Nephandi would be pragmatic enough to &quot;take him in&quot;. Its either that or he stays hidden in the Traditions, even helping them fight the Nephandi to show what a good person he is (Assuming he wants the protection of a group, and is not interested in full independence)<br /> <br /> Think of real life politics. United States style. It's not a perfect analogy, because Nephandi are not half the awakened, but it works. Bob is a centrist. He is useful to help you get to that<br /> electoral college majority <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> . Therefore, the side that is more pragmatic about it will &quot;win&quot; him over.<br /> And yes, some &quot;Bob&quot; may choose to become independent, because that's how it works, albeit with the caveat that the Traditions may hunt him down, thus forcing the choice on him.<br /> <br /> The reason i wrote all this is because i think the OP can/should be able to play the Nephandi, but i am pointing out that the canonical interpretation of the Nephandi is, in my personal opinion, problematic, because the current political makeup of Mages only takes into account some very specific shades of gran (and black of course)<br /> I do believe that the reason for this is that the writers knew that a player is more likely to want to play &quot;Bob&quot; rather than an omnicidal maniac, and that they tried to make it, not impossible per se, but rather hard to do so. (short of the storyteller choosing to houserule the morality of the Traditions,<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> *For the purpose of this hypothesis, let us ignore, at least for now, the issue of the Caul. Let's say that when various infernalist started uniting Jodi Blake had failed in convincing &quot;fucking all of them&quot; in visiting the Cauls.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-11T02:51:44-07:00","id":1508010,"title":"","content":"On reflection playing an regular infernalist is more interesting and that's before you consider just playing a guy who simply studies demonology for altruistic reasons.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-11T05:40:21-07:00","id":1508020,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1507223-what-is-the-problem-with-nephandi?p=1508009#post1508009\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, you can become an independent mage. That is definitely an option.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's pretty much the only option if you don't care about following other people's rules.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The crafts are not. I am, say, a Hermetic/Etherite so joining the wu lung is simply not a viable thing for me. </div> </div> </div>You say that like either of those traditions aren't full of amoral assholes. The Hermetics are some of the least moral mages in the setting and their love of pushing magical boundaries is like 99% of the justification for the Order of Reason to gather together to remove mystic mages from ruling over humans. The Etherites have a whole faction of mad scientists where Frankenstein and Herbert West would be considered dull.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, remember that i am not talking about &quot;well intentioned extremist&quot; kind of evil, or even hypocritical evil. </div> </div> </div>Given your Bob examples, I have no idea what kind of &quot;evil&quot; you have in mind other than, &quot;hypothetical evil designed perfectly to win and Internet debate that has no actual appeal as a character to play or consider as a literary figure.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I assume that mages who are just immoral/amoral (but not unstable) are more, in terms of quantity, than those whose dream is to undo the freaking universe. </div> </div> </div>I think you're giving the letter of Tradition law too much credit here. Any character with the creativity to be a mage has the creativity to be exceedingly immoral/amoral by human standards without running afoul of Tradtion law.<br /> <br /> The only two relevant to dealing with Sleepers are:<br /> <br /> That Tradition mages are supposed to protect Sleepers; though given that the Euthanatoi amongst many others are allowed to operate freely, this is clearly not &quot;do no harm to Sleepers.&quot; Anything you can justify as for the good of Sleepers by protecting them from their own bad people is clearly logic that works in Tradition law. Dexter Morgan who kills killers is fine as a Tradition mage because he's protecting Sleepers by removing the small number of serial killers out there from the population to save hundreds of lives... even if it's just because they enjoy it.<br /> <br /> And that Tradition mages aren't supposed to go vulgar in front of Sleepers. Creating giant flesh monsters out of the brain-dead bodies of murderers and rapists that you've incapacitated? Fine, just don't let it out of its cage.<br /> <br /> The Protocols are not nearly as &quot;goody goody&quot; as you seem to think they are.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That is, modern &quot;House of Tremere&quot; mages would be a bit more discreet, a bit more restrained, but still there.&quot; </div> </div> </div>This is already the default way things are in the Traditions. The Cult of Ecstasy has a whole internal schism around whether it's OK to force altered states on Sleepers in attempts to broaden their experiences and perceptions of reality. Some think that non-consensual acts inherently cause more harm than good and taint spreading enlightenment via practices that change your view of the world, and the other think that consent is just a trap that keeps Sleepers sleeping and they need someone to snap them out of it so they can really understand the world and the idea of informed consent when sending people on magic fueled drug trips is laughable.<br /> <br /> As far as the laws of the Traditions go, neither side is inherently running afoul of the 7th Protocol, so it's left as an internal matter.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> B)This compromise would be refused and a lot of these people would instead be pushed into an alliance with the Nephandi. </div> </div> </div>This is also part of the setting as well. Most Nephandi are recruited from other factions. How exactly do you think the Nephandi can do that if there aren't individuals immoral enough to tempt towards Descent? And the Nephandi don't really care. Either they create another true believer and they're that much closer to dragging the world into oblivion, or they gain another sacrificial &quot;Nephandus&quot; pawn to let the other factions think they're doing a good job at stopping the Nephandi by taking a few out.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Evil as hell, right? That is, i really don't want to get close to this guy. I assume that even the first option (with the rapists/serial killers) would be some level of illegal in most Traditions. </div> </div> </div>It's not inherently illegal in any of them, or the over arching rules all the Traditions agreed to. It wouldn't make you very popular in some of them, but those are the less inclined towards this particular kind of effort. The response is going to be more from the perspective of, &quot;why are you wasting you time creating pointless flesh-abominations&quot; rather than, &quot;how dare you treat evil Sleepers as test subjects.&quot; Keep in mind that the laws that govern internal matters within each Tradition are subject to the fact that most of them don't have any centralized body to enforce them. The leaders of the Cultists can say that the Code of Ananda is the rule of the Tradition, but nothing within the Tradition's laws actually lets them do anything to all the schismatics that don't follow the Code besides not let them have access to various magical resources.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But the point is that Bob exists. </div> </div> </div>And the Bobs of the Mage world tend to get killed. Usually by becoming disposable assets to factions that are more tolerant of their inclinations as long as they can be useful later. The Progenitors would want to try to recruit your specific Bob too, even if they know he's incapable of being mutual, he can generate some good data before he's turned into the experimental subject.<br /> <br /> It's important to remember that Mage is a game where belief is really important to making compelling mage characters. Characters that don't have strong beliefs and just do things because it feels good (whatever the moral judgement on those actions is) tend not to make successful mages. That's why there's way more &quot;well intentioned extremist&quot; type &quot;evil&quot; mages in the game. They have fully formed philosophies behind their actions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How on earth will his every day actions be different compared to a Nephandus trying to achieve (personal) Descension? </div> </div> </div>This is why there aren't any Bobs in the game though. Bob isn't a fully realized character. &quot;All the evil of a Nephandus without and of the convictions of a Nephandus,&quot; isn't something worth exploring in the game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Think of real life politics. United States style. It's not a perfect analogy, because Nephandi are not half the awakened, but it works. Bob is a centrist. He is useful to help you get to that<br /> electoral college majority <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> . Therefore, the side that is more pragmatic about it will &quot;win&quot; him over. </div> </div> </div>The problem is that the &quot;centrist&quot; vote in politics is not actually a vote governed by rational and logical decision making. Centrists are just as prone to emotional election picks as either right or left. It's just a different emotional case you need to make.<br /> <br /> <br /> And yes, some &quot;Bob&quot; may choose to become independent, because that's how it works, albeit with the caveat that the Traditions may hunt him down, thus forcing the choice on him.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The reason i wrote all this is because i think the OP can/should be able to play the Nephandi,... </div> </div> </div>Nobody is stopping them. But the OP also didn't say they were looking to be sold on this idea, but was curious why so many people are against it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...but i am pointing out that the canonical interpretation of the Nephandi is, in my personal opinion, problematic, because the current political makeup of Mages only takes into account some very specific shades of gran (and black of course) </div> </div> </div>You need to do a lot more work justifying things from the material in the books. Right now, as previously assessed, your stance is based on an extremely myopic view of the setting as presented.<br /> <br /> Like this:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I do believe that the reason for this is that the writers knew that a player is more likely to want to play &quot;Bob&quot; rather than an omnicidal maniac, and that they tried to make it, not impossible per se, but rather hard to do so. (short of the storyteller choosing to houserule the morality of the Traditions, </div> </div> </div>The writers that included the Euthanatos... a Tradition that most other Traditions view as a bunch of Dexter Morgans - at best - and who's internal rules demand that they act as vigilante killers.<br /> <br /> The writers that included the Mad Scientists in the Society of Ether? The writers that made sure the laws of the Cult of Ecstasy doesn't let them kick out the Aghoris, Acharne, or Hagalaz? That the Dreamspeakers have active terrorists cells among their ranks?<br /> <br /> The reasons is like I said: they want mages,and Bob isn't really a mage, because he doesn't have a fully formed belief system. He's just a hypothetical guy that's evil and can do magic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-12T06:55:05-07:00","id":1508149,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Conradine</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1507223-what-is-the-problem-with-nephandi?p=1507880#post1507880\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I found a mention about some Nephandi searching some kind of redemption. Beside the question &quot;is redemption possible or not&quot;, since the Caul literally inverts and break the soul, is redemption something that a Nephandi can even conceptualize?</div> </div> </div> </div>To be honest, i think the only &quot;canon&quot; i have ever found about that is by using spirit 9 (and even that is just a logical extrapolation) or something ridiculous like that. In regards to how Nephandi conceptualize redemption/if they do...<br /> <br /> I like to use Stephen Lea's interpretation of the Caul.<br /> <br /> So, right of the caul you are &quot;normal&quot;. You still do not wish to hurt your grandma. But the voice of conscience is inverted. If you do hurt your loved one it will not say &quot; why did you do that/don't do it&quot; rather the whisper will be &quot;Do it again until it no longer hurts&quot;. Thus, a Nephandi asking for help can be pretty believable.. because right now he genuinely feels bad about what he did..but the internal introspection is likely to lead to a different conclusion!<br /> <br /> (I would, personally, in the context of a vampire game, literally give the Nephandi an anti-conscience virtue if that makes representing them easier.)<br /> <br /> So, making a Nephandus &quot;grow&quot; as a person, ironically produces a monster, whereas making him more venal and &quot;lazy&quot; i.e making him fall from his path, may give you something remotely normal. But that person can still find it deep within him to surprise you with some great monstrous act, just as the most venal among as can sometimes surprise us with some great virtuous one.<br /> <br /> In short, they can conceptualize it, in my opinion, but the results are problematic to say the least.<br /> Add to this the ingrained prejudiced against inverted individuals and, well... there is probably not much good will on either side<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}